%I A401781 #14 Nov 26 2026 16:11:27
%S A401781 0,0,0,1,0,14,49,300,1591,9402,54978,339764,2136030,14546877,99427954,
%T A401781 715660119
%N A401781 Number of set partitions of the cyclic group Z_n having no transversal whose consecutive cyclic distances are a permutation of the block sizes.
%C A401781 In the sense of transversal theory (Mirsky, 1971), a transversal of a set partition is a system of distinct representatives, containing exactly one element from each block.
%C A401781 Elements of a transversal of a partition P of Z_n into k parts, arranged in cyclic order around the circle, have distances between consecutive elements forming a sequence of k positive integers necessarily summing to n - an integer composition of n.
%C A401781 A transversal is compatible if these consecutive cyclic distances are a permutation of the block sizes of P.
%C A401781 The sequence a(n) counts the set partitions of Z_n that have no such compatible transversal.
%C A401781 The existence of a compatible transversal is an invariant under the dihedral group D_n (rotations and reflections). Consequently the dihedral orbits of set partitions (A084708, Fripertinger 1997) partition cleanly into solvable orbits and obstruction orbits. The value a(n) is the sum of the orbit sizes of the obstruction orbits.
%C A401781 The number of obstruction dihedral orbits for n = 1..8 is 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 4, 7, 41, out of A084708(n) = 1, 2, 3, 7, 12, 37, 93, 354 total dihedral orbits.
%C A401781 Sum of all orbit sizes is the Bell number B(n) = A000110(n). The number of partitions with at least one compatible transversal is B(n) - a(n).
%C A401781 The first obstruction occurs at n = 4 (a(4) = 1) for the partition {{1, 3}, {2, 4}} as two diagonals. Their block sizes are (2, 2), requiring distance 2 between the two transversal points (i.e., antipodal points). And since both antipodal pairs are grouped into single blocks, any transversal must choose one point from each block (e.g., {1, 2}), yielding cyclic distances 1 and 3 instead of 2 and 2.
%C A401781 For n = 1, 2, 3, 5 all partitions have at least one compatible transversal, so a(n) = 0.
%C A401781 Obstructions occur for all n >= 6.
%C A401781 Computed via an exhaustive parallel census through n = 16 (evaluating 12086676030 set partitions).
%D A401781 H. Fripertinger, Enumeration of isometry classes of mosaics, Discrete Math., 176 (1997), 87-98.
%D A401781 L. Mirsky, Transversal Theory, Academic Press, 1971.
%H A401781 Paul Sampson, <a href="https://github.com/applutan/isotypic-transversals">Isotypic Composition Transversals on Cyclic Lattices</a>, GitHub.
%e A401781 For n = 4: Bell number B_4 = 15; 7 dihedral orbits (A084708).
%e A401781   Of the 7 dihedral orbits, 6 have a compatible transversal.
%e A401781   The sole obstruction orbit has size 1, represented by P = {{1, 3}, {2, 4}} of block sizes (2, 2). Any transversal picks one element from each block, giving the pairs {1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}, or {1, 4}, all of which have cyclic distances (1, 3). No transversal can achieve cyclic distances (2, 2). Thus a(4) = 1.
%e A401781 For n = 6: Bell number B_6 = 203; 37 dihedral orbits (A084708).
%e A401781   Exactly 4 dihedral orbits (comprising 14 labeled partitions, a(6) = 14) admit no compatible transversal:
%e A401781   - 1 orbit of size 3 with block sizes (4, 1, 1), represented by {{1}, {4}, {2, 3, 5, 6}}: the two singletons {1} and {4} are antipodal (distance 3 apart), so any transversal containing both cannot have consecutive distance 4.
%e A401781   - 1 orbit of size 2 with block sizes (3, 1, 1, 1), represented by {{1}, {3}, {5}, {2, 4, 6}}: the three singletons {1}, {3}, {5} must all be chosen, forcing all consecutive distances to be at most 2, so distance 3 can never be achieved.
%e A401781   - 2 orbits of sizes 6 and 3 with block sizes (2, 2, 2), represented by {{1, 2}, {3, 5}, {4, 6}} and {{1, 3}, {2, 5}, {4, 6}}. To achieve cyclic distances (2, 2, 2), a transversal would need to be the equilateral triangle {1, 3, 5} or {2, 4, 6}, but in each case the triangle contains two elements from the same block.
%o A401781 (Python)
%o A401781 from itertools import permutations
%o A401781 def partitions(s):
%o A401781     if not s: yield []; return
%o A401781     for p in partitions(s[1:]):
%o A401781         for i in range(len(p)): yield p[:i] + [p[i] + [s[0]]] + p[i+1:]
%o A401781         yield [[s[0]]] + p
%o A401781 def a(n):
%o A401781     cnt = 0
%o A401781     for p in partitions(list(range(1, n + 1))):
%o A401781         k, sz, bsets = len(p), tuple(sorted(len(b) for b in p)), [set(b) for b in p]
%o A401781         ok = False
%o A401781         for c in set(permutations(sz)):
%o A401781             for o in range(1, n + 1):
%o A401781                 pts, cur = [], o
%o A401781                 for st in c: pts.append(cur); cur = (cur - 1 + st) % n + 1
%o A401781                 if len(set(pts)) == k and all(sum(x in b for x in pts) == 1 for b in bsets):
%o A401781                     ok = True; break
%o A401781             if ok: break
%o A401781         if not ok: cnt += 1
%o A401781     return cnt
%o A401781 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 7)])
%Y A401781 Cf. A000110 (Bell numbers B_n).
%Y A401781 Cf. A084708 (Dihedral orbits of set partitions).
%Y A401781 Cf. A401780 (Canonical composition transversals).
%K A401781 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A401781 1,6
